"Machine Learning (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new)\nciteturn41250view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new\",\"lineno\":6}); Total lines: 5005\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8:   * cite5†New submissions L9:   * cite6†Cross-lists L10:   * cite7†Replacements L11: \nL12: See cite8†recent articles\nL13: \nL14: ### Showing new listings for Monday, 19 October 2026\nL15: \nL16: Total of 391 entries\nL17: \nL18: Showing up to 2000 entries per page: cite9†fewer | more | all\nL19: ### New submissions (showing 126 of 126 entries)\nL20: \nL21: [1] cite10†arXiv:2610.22379 [cite11†pdf , cite12†html , cite13†other ]\nL22: \nL23: \nL24: Title: When is a large language model a good calibration agent? Physics-labeled prompting for budgeted engineering simulator calibration\nL25: \nL26: cite14†Ruo-Qian Wang L27: \nL28: Comments: Submitted to Engineering Application of Artificial Intelligence\nL29: \nL30: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL31: Calibrating expensive engineering simulators requires tuning model parameters to match reference data, a process heavily constrained by computational costs. This study investigates whether Large Language Models (LLMs), conditioned on physical diagnostic descriptions of simulation errors, can propose informative parameter updates more efficiently than conventional derivative-free optimizers. We test this on a cylinder-wake Large-Eddy Simulation and a damped-oscillator inverse problem.\nL32: Physics-labeled prompts are evaluated against four text ablations (removed, anonymized, padded, misdirected) and standard optimizers (Nelder-Mead, Bayesian optimization, CMA-ES, and random search). Results demonstrate a strong early semantic advantage: the first physics-informed LLM proposal improves the initial design in 23 of 25 trials. After five evaluations, the median calibration error is 21.9 times lower than physics-free controls.\nL33: However, this advantage is transient - disappearing by fifteen evaluations - and inherent LLM decoder variability yields a 9.8-fold performance spread. To address this, we introduce a hybrid workflow that uses the LLM as a warm-start, passing accumulated evaluations to a conventional optimizer. This two-stage numerical continuation policy reaches target accuracy in just 9 simulator runs, outperforming the LLM alone (12 runs), Nelder-Mead (17 runs), and Bayesian optimization (>60 runs).\nL34: Supported by sensitivity analysis validating the test cases, this hybrid physics-labeled LLM scheme proves highly effective as a calibration agent under strict evaluation budgets.\nL35: [2] cite15†arXiv:2610.22380 [cite16†pdf , cite17†html , cite18†other ]\nL36: \nL37: \nL38: Title: Trust but Verify: Designing Region-Inhomogeneous Forward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion\nL39: \nL40: cite19†Gunmay Jhingran L41: \nL42: Comments: Accepted to 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026). Workshop: AI for Stochastic Dynamics. 10 pages\nL43: \nL44: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL45: Continuous diffusion treats the forward process as a design space; discrete language diffusion often uses absorbing corruption, leaving context fully trusted throughout generation. This makes trust binary: corrupted context is copied as ground truth, propagating errors into generated spans. We reopen this design space by combining absorbing and uniform-mixing kernels with region-dependent rates.\nL46: Missing positions follow absorbing dynamics, while context undergoes low-rate random substitution, exposing the model to observed but uncertain tokens during training. At inference, a heuristic sampler re-injects noise into suspicious context under an annealed intensity and freezes context updates late in the trajectory. An 8,000-step finetune of a pretrained 170M-parameter masked diffusion model takes under five hours on a 6 GB laptop GPU.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArtificial Intelligence (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.AI/new)\nciteturn41250view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.AI/new\",\"lineno\":6}); Total lines: 4424\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8:   * cite5†New submissions L9:   * cite6†Cross-lists L10:   * cite7†Replacements L11: \nL12: See cite8†recent articles\nL13: \nL14: ### Showing new listings for Monday, 19 October 2026\nL15: \nL16: Total of 344 entries\nL17: \nL18: Showing up to 2000 entries per page: cite9†fewer | more | all\nL19: ### New submissions (showing 92 of 92 entries)\nL20: \nL21: [1] cite10†arXiv:2610.22373 [cite11†pdf , cite12†html , cite13†other ]\nL22: \nL23: \nL24: Title: Structured Authority Injection: Persistent Human Control for LLM Agents via Typed MCP Overrides\nL25: \nL26: cite14†Ryan Tran , cite15†YueMing Zhang , cite16†Lingfeng Tao , cite17†Nan Yang , cite18†Shengjie Xu , cite19†Yu Yin , cite18†Shuai Xu , cite20†Wenlu Zhang , cite21†Hailu Xu L27: \nL28: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL29: LLM-based agents are increasingly used as autonomous orchestrators in distributed and multi-agent systems, yet a control gap remains: no protocol-level mechanism allows human operators to inject binding directives that persist across planning cycles. Free-text overrides suffer from ambiguous interpretation due to lack of schema validation and from authority erosion as operator instructions lose influence within accumulating context.\nL30: We propose Structured Authority Injection (SAI), a protocol that addresses these issues by routing operator corrections through typed, schema-validated Model Context Protocol (MCP) tool calls and maintaining a ranked authority context prepended to each LLM prompt. SAI defines three override classes (Constraint, Priority, and Strategy) with distinct persistence semantics and an explicit precedence order enforced via a directed authority channel inaccessible to the planning process.\nL31: The method requires no model fine-tuning or access to model weights and applies to instruction-tuned LLMs supporting MCP tool use. Evaluation across nine LLMs and eight scenarios targeting agent failure modes shows reduced ambiguous directive interpretation and mitigated authority decay across planning iterations, with lower prompt overhead in the strongest models (up to 38% depending on model and scenario), though benefit varies substantially across models.\nL32: SAI provides a general design pattern for agent systems requiring explicit and persistent human control.\nL33: [2] cite22†arXiv:2610.22400 [cite23†pdf , cite24†html , cite25†other ]\nL34: \nL35: \nL36: Title: Rethinking User Encoders: Routing Diffusion States for Scenario-Adaptive User Representation\nL37: \nL38: cite26†Junru Zhang , cite27†Jinyong Wen , cite28†Bin Dou , cite29†Wuliang Huang , cite30†Baokun Wang , cite31†Yu Cheng , cite32†Weiqiang Wang , cite33†Cheng Peng L39: \nL40: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL41: Existing large language model (LLM)-based user encoders serialize heterogeneous behavioral logs and compress them into static representations through autoregressive (AR) prediction. However, their unidirectional constraint limits bidirectional information flow, hindering cross-modal interactions and the joint preservation of multi-source signals. Consequently, the learned static representations struggle to accommodate the distinct evidence requirements of diverse downstream scenarios.\nL42: We address these limitations by unlocking the iterative denoising process of diffusion language models (dLLMs) as a multi-granularity reconstruction space. Based on this insight, we propose FINDERS (FactorIzatioN of Diffusion statEs for Routing across Scenarios), a router-equipped dLLM framework comprising four operations: denoise, factorize, route, and compress. Learnable meta-slots organize complementary denoising states into transferable cross-modal semantics and modality-specific behavioral factors.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMachine Learning (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new)\nciteturn41250view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new\",\"pattern\":\"Replacements\"}); Total lines: 5005\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8:   * cite5†New submissions L9:   * cite6†Cross-lists L10:   * cite7†Replacements L11: \nL12: See cite8†recent articles\nL13: \nL14: ### Showing new listings for Monday, 19 October 2026\nL15: \nL16: Total of 391 entries\nL17: \nL18: Showing up to 2000 entries per page: cite9†fewer | more | all\nL19: ### New submissions (showing 126 of 126 entries)\nL20: \nL21: [1] cite10†arXiv:2610.22379 [cite11†pdf , cite12†html , cite13†other ]\nL22: \nL23: \nL24: Title: When is a large language model a good calibration agent? Physics-labeled prompting for budgeted engineering simulator calibration\nL25: \nL26: cite14†Ruo-Qian Wang L27: \nL28: Comments: Submitted to Engineering Application of Artificial Intelligence\nL29: \nL30: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL31: Calibrating expensive engineering simulators requires tuning model parameters to match reference data, a process heavily constrained by computational costs. This study investigates whether Large Language Models (LLMs), conditioned on physical diagnostic descriptions of simulation errors, can propose informative parameter updates more efficiently than conventional derivative-free optimizers. We test this on a cylinder-wake Large-Eddy Simulation and a damped-oscillator inverse problem.\nL32: Physics-labeled prompts are evaluated against four text ablations (removed, anonymized, padded, misdirected) and standard optimizers (Nelder-Mead, Bayesian optimization, CMA-ES, and random search). Results demonstrate a strong early semantic advantage: the first physics-informed LLM proposal improves the initial design in 23 of 25 trials. After five evaluations, the median calibration error is 21.9 times lower than physics-free controls.\nL33: However, this advantage is transient - disappearing by fifteen evaluations - and inherent LLM decoder variability yields a 9.8-fold performance spread. To address this, we introduce a hybrid workflow that uses the LLM as a warm-start, passing accumulated evaluations to a conventional optimizer. This two-stage numerical continuation policy reaches target accuracy in just 9 simulator runs, outperforming the LLM alone (12 runs), Nelder-Mead (17 runs), and Bayesian optimization (>60 runs).\nL34: Supported by sensitivity analysis validating the test cases, this hybrid physics-labeled LLM scheme proves highly effective as a calibration agent under strict evaluation budgets.\nL35: [2] cite15†arXiv:2610.22380 [cite16†pdf , cite17†html , cite18†other ]\nL36: \nL37: \nL38: Title: Trust but Verify: Designing Region-Inhomogeneous Forward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion\nL39: \nL40: cite19†Gunmay Jhingran L41: \nL42: Comments: Accepted to 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026). Workshop: AI for Stochastic Dynamics. 10 pages\nL43: \nL44: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL2434: It brings twice as much as benefits a good choice of backbone on competing-risks data, and no more than it on single-event data: the loss is decisive for competing risks. The backbone matters too, and must match the modality: tabular networks lead on tables, pretrained vision encoders on images. The two choices compound rather than substitute.\nL2435: Proper scoring rules reveal these dynamics; the concordance index (C-index), which is not proper, does not separate models: neither axis moves it, not even tuning on the loss built to improve it. This is an avenue of progress: composed, the two give a model nobody has tried, RealMLP or TabM trained with the cumulative-incidence loss, which takes the best Brier score in our benchmark.\nL2436: [191] cite1540†arXiv:2610.23372 (cross-list from cs.RO) [cite1541†pdf , cite1542†html , cite1543†other ]\nL2437: \nL2438: \nL2439: Title: Beyond Success Rate: Interventional Evaluation of Memory in Vision-Language-Action Systems\nL2440: \nL2441: cite1544†Keyuan Luo , cite966†Yi Xie , cite1545†Wanyun Ling , cite1546†Yuanzhe Li , cite219†Ziyue Li , cite1547†Zhuoqi Zeng L2442: \nL2443: Comments: 41 pages, including references and appendices\nL2444: \nL2445: Subjects: Robotics (cs.RO); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL2446: When we say that a robot uses memory, does that memory actually play a role? Success-rate ablations alone cannot establish the use of task-relevant facts: they change information and computation together, while aggregate rates can hide offsetting gains and losses.\nL2447: We propose an Interventional Memory Evaluation Protocol (IMEP) for vision-language-action (VLA) systems to address four questions: (1) Does memory provide a net benefit? (2) Under what conditions does memory help? (3) Do changes to memory alter task outcomes? (4) Are these effects specific to task-relevant facts? At the retrieval interface, we compare target-fact substitutions with fact-preserving replacements, minimal numerical perturbations, and exact replicas, while assessing decision correctness separately from motor execution success.\nL2448: We evaluate within-episode, cross-episode, and agentic memory. In cross-episode learned-bank experiments, a one-unit-in-the-last-place (ULP) intervention, including its installation procedure, changes 11.9% of seed-paired outcomes, while changing key facts produces no clear additional effect over fact-preserving replacements. To examine when memory helps, we introduce ChainBench, where correct decisions require historical information unavailable in current observations.\nL2449: The full memory configuration raises task success from 39.1% to 74.3%, matching the oracle-decision condition under shared recorded executions. These results show that correct history can support robot decisions, but establishing whether a memory system realizes this benefit requires fact-specific evidence beyond aggregate success rates.\nL2450: [192] cite1548†arXiv:2610.23383 (cross-list from cs.PF) [cite1549†pdf , cite1550†html , cite1551†other ]\nL2451: \nL2452: \nL2453: Title: Data-Driven Prediction of Optimal MPI Rank Placement and Node Count for Memory-Bound CFD on AMD EPYC Clusters\nL2454: \nL2455: cite1552†Marcin Lawenda , cite1553†Łukasz Szustak , cite1554†Aleksandra Krasicka L2456: \nL2457: Comments: 15 pages, 3 figures, 5 tables\nL2458: \nL2459: Subjects: Performance (cs.PF); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL2460: Users who run a memory-bound OpenFOAM case on an AMD EPYC cluster must decide how many nodes to use and how many MPI ranks to place on each core complex. The fastest choice is often not the cheapest one. This paper revisits our earlier analytical model of this performance and energy trade-off with 560 measured configurations of the motorBike case on five Zen 4 and Zen 5 EPYC processors, including socket power from processor telemetry.\nL2461: On up to four nodes, small meshes are both fastest and cheapest on a single node on most processors. For the largest mesh the largest node count is both the fastest and the cheapest option, because its speedup is superlinear. A genuine trade-off arises only for medium meshes, where full speed costs a median of 20 to 97% more node hours than the cheapest option. The L3 miss rate and the halo traffic per cell are consistent with this pattern.\nL2462: For a new mesh of the same case, the configuration that was optimal on the nearest measured mesh is near optimal in 75 to 85% of the decisions. Running the two configurations it recommends for speed and for cost raises this share to 85 to 90% for runtime and node hours and determines the trade-off with median errors of 3 to 5 percentage points. Neither gradient boosted trees trained on the same data nor the earlier polynomial model improves on this simple procedure.\nL2463: [193] cite1555†arXiv:2610.23388 (cross-list from cs.CL) [cite1556†pdf , cite1557†html , cite1558†other ]\nL2464: \nL2465: \nL2466: Title: Sentence-Level Topic Modeling with Lexical and Contextual Evidence\nL2467: \nL2468: cite1559†Federica Granese , cite1560†Serena Villata , cite1561†Charles Bouveyron L2469: \nL2470: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL2471: Topic modeling faces a fundamental granularity dilemma: probabilistic topic models assign topics at the word level, whereas transformer-based methods operate at the document level. The latter overlooks that real-world documents typically cover multiple topics, whereas the former relies on individual words, which rarely provide sufficient context to identify them. We introduce SentenceETM, a neural topic model that assigns a topic to each sentence by jointly using lexical and contextual evidence.\nL2472: Each topic is described both by a distribution over the vocabulary, defined through word embeddings, and by a Gaussian over sentence embeddings concentrated on a topic-specific low-dimensional subspace. The topic of a sentence generates both its words and its embedding and is marginalized exactly, so that its posterior combines the two sources of evidence. Document proportions are inferred with amortized variational inference, while the Gaussian parameters are re-estimated in closed form.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41250view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new\",\"pattern\":\"22454\"}); Total lines: 2886\nL31: In a small language model the vocabulary is the single largest part. In Gemma 3 270M the embedding table holds 168M of 268M parameters, and because quantisation methods leave the embedding in 16-bit, it is also most of the file once the rest of the model is compressed. We ask what that table actually buys.\nL32: We replace it with one frozen table of 512-bit codes, 16 MB, with no trainable parameters, derived once from a pretrained embedding by principal-component projection and sign binarisation, and we train the same Gemma-shaped body from scratch on the same 2.05B tokens with and without it. The learned table buys 0.20 nats of validation loss for 2.6 times the trainable parameters, but on LAMBADA, HellaSwag and PIQA the frozen-table model is better or tied; only ARC-Easy favours the learned table.\nL33: With a ternary BitNet b1.58 body the whole model is a 46 MB file, still ties the float model on LAMBADA and PIQA, and on a laptop CPU generates 615 to 740 tokens per second in 62 MB of memory, five to six times the speed of the float model's 8-bit GGUF in cite17†this http URL†llama.cpp at a seventh of the memory.\nL34: The frozen table wins by 1.7 to 2.3 nats on rare words, accepts 10,000 new words after training with a 3% cost where the learned table collapses by 43%, and all of it is released with the weights, the data and every evaluation answer.\nL35: [2] cite18†arXiv:2610.22454 [cite19†pdf , cite20†html , cite21†other ]\nL36: \nL37: \nL38: Title: Where Prefill Expert Reduction Hurts MoE Models: A Few Prompt Tokens Carry Most of the Short-Answer F1 Loss\nL39: \nL40: cite22†Zhiyuan Wang L41: \nL42: Comments: 8 pages main text, 26 pages with appendices; supplementary material in ancillary files\nL43: \nL44: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL45: Running prompt tokens through fewer routed experts during prefill is a simple way to make mixture-of-experts (MoE) serving cheaper, yet it is unclear what this damages. We locate the damage with token-, layer- and attention-path interventions on short-answer question answering in four MoE families. At half the experts the F1 loss of three families is mostly answer form: the model still finds the answer but stops answering briefly.\nL46: In Qwen3-30B-A3B this loss is made at the two chat-template tokens before the answer, and in DeepSeek-V2-Lite at the first 8-32 prompt tokens, where its instruction sits; by answer likelihood these positions account for about half and a fifth of the damage. Reduction changes the first token's attention mass, norm and largest activation by less than 4%, and restoring super experts or massive activations recovers at most about a quarter of the loss, well below keeping the sensitive positions full.\nL47: On real prompts with about 4% of tokens at full experts, placing them on the task interface keeps F1 within one point of the unmodified model, while placing them at random in the documents is about 3 points worse (two of five prespecified primary comparisons; the others were undecided or not run).\nL48: Because the answer boundary always ends a single-turn prompt, and DeepSeek's loss was no longer detectable once its instruction moved away from the start, the sensitive tokens lay at the prompt ends in every sensitive layout we found, so rules that keep the ends are not distinguished from rules that read application marks. Time to first token falls about 14% on 11K-token prompts and not at 2K tokens.\nL49: [3] cite23†arXiv:2610.22468 [cite24†pdf , cite25†html , cite26†other ]\nL50: \nL51: \nL52: Title: From Parallels to Divergence: Language Models' Learning of Action Nominal Constructions\nL53: \nL54: cite27†Iskar Deng , cite28†Shane Steinert-Threlkeld L55: \nL56: Comments: 41 pages, including appendices\nL57: \nL58: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL59: Action nominal constructions (ANCs) express events in nominal form, with their argument marking following patterns found in clauses or ordinary noun phrases. In this paper, we investigate how different grammatical configurations affect language models' learning of ANCs and related constructions, and how these learning preferences compare with typological distributions in natural languages.\nL60: We use a grammar-engineered method for synthetic corpus construction that combines shared semantic input from natural corpora with computational grammars. We construct 96 target languages varying in clause word order, noun-phrase word order, alignment, complement system, and ANC strategy. For each language, we train GPT-2-small models from scratch and evaluate them using targeted minimal pairs covering 34 grammatical phenomena.\nL61: The results show typological parallels in basic clause configurations but divergences in complement systems and ANC strategies, with models also learning combinations rare or unattested in natural languages. This partial alignment suggests that natural language distributions may reflect both general learning pressures and functional demands not fully captured by model learning experiments.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMachine Learning (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new)\nciteturn41250view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new\",\"pattern\":\"22380\"}); Total lines: 5005\nL31: Calibrating expensive engineering simulators requires tuning model parameters to match reference data, a process heavily constrained by computational costs. This study investigates whether Large Language Models (LLMs), conditioned on physical diagnostic descriptions of simulation errors, can propose informative parameter updates more efficiently than conventional derivative-free optimizers. We test this on a cylinder-wake Large-Eddy Simulation and a damped-oscillator inverse problem.\nL32: Physics-labeled prompts are evaluated against four text ablations (removed, anonymized, padded, misdirected) and standard optimizers (Nelder-Mead, Bayesian optimization, CMA-ES, and random search). Results demonstrate a strong early semantic advantage: the first physics-informed LLM proposal improves the initial design in 23 of 25 trials. After five evaluations, the median calibration error is 21.9 times lower than physics-free controls.\nL33: However, this advantage is transient - disappearing by fifteen evaluations - and inherent LLM decoder variability yields a 9.8-fold performance spread. To address this, we introduce a hybrid workflow that uses the LLM as a warm-start, passing accumulated evaluations to a conventional optimizer. This two-stage numerical continuation policy reaches target accuracy in just 9 simulator runs, outperforming the LLM alone (12 runs), Nelder-Mead (17 runs), and Bayesian optimization (>60 runs).\nL34: Supported by sensitivity analysis validating the test cases, this hybrid physics-labeled LLM scheme proves highly effective as a calibration agent under strict evaluation budgets.\nL35: [2] cite15†arXiv:2610.22380 [cite16†pdf , cite17†html , cite18†other ]\nL36: \nL37: \nL38: Title: Trust but Verify: Designing Region-Inhomogeneous Forward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion\nL39: \nL40: cite19†Gunmay Jhingran L41: \nL42: Comments: Accepted to 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026). Workshop: AI for Stochastic Dynamics. 10 pages\nL43: \nL44: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL45: Continuous diffusion treats the forward process as a design space; discrete language diffusion often uses absorbing corruption, leaving context fully trusted throughout generation. This makes trust binary: corrupted context is copied as ground truth, propagating errors into generated spans. We reopen this design space by combining absorbing and uniform-mixing kernels with region-dependent rates.\nL46: Missing positions follow absorbing dynamics, while context undergoes low-rate random substitution, exposing the model to observed but uncertain tokens during training. At inference, a heuristic sampler re-injects noise into suspicious context under an annealed intensity and freezes context updates late in the trajectory. An 8,000-step finetune of a pretrained 170M-parameter masked diffusion model takes under five hours on a 6 GB laptop GPU.\nL47: The base model assigns roughly 50% probability to observed tokens even when they are uniform-random garbage. For uniform-random corruption, finetuning detects corrupted context with 0.96 recall and 0.91 precision, improves infill token-F1 by 3.8 points at 10% corruption and 6.4 points at 20%, and removes the accuracy dip next to corruptions. Clean conditioning shows no measurable regression, with a false-edit rate below 0.4%.\nL48: Gains for plausible corruptions are weaker: about 0.5 F1 points at 10% corruption, without statistical significance. Our results suggest that detectability and damage depend jointly on departure from the data conditional: nearby noise is hard to detect and semantically mild, while distant noise is conspicuous and destructive. We interpret absorbing diffusion as the infinite-contrast limit of a trust axis, analogous to the signal-to-noise gap between known and unknown regions in diffusion inpainting.\nL49: In our setting of a 170M model and a single text domain, intermediate trust is reached by changing forward dynamics rather than architecture.\nL50: [3] cite20†arXiv:2610.22381 [cite21†pdf , cite22†html , cite23†other ]\nL51: \nL52: \nL53: Title: Binding-Specific Relearning Savings for Test-Time Continual Learning\nL54: \nL55: cite24†Zhe Zhou L56: \nL57: Comments: Accepted at the NeurIPS 2026 Workshop on Towards Test-Time Continual Learning Agents (TTCL). 15 pages, 2 figures. Code: cite25†this https URL†github.com L58: \nL59: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL60: When a fact becomes harder to elicit from a language model, has the model lost it, or has it merely become less accessible? Parameter-updating test-time continual learning agents are usually evaluated by current output accuracy; we ask whether that measurement captures how prior experience shapes later updates. We test whether learning a specific factual binding produces a later relearning advantage beyond familiarity with its entity, answer vocabulary, format, or query slot.\nL61: In Qwen2.5-1.5B, we compare three matched, counterbalanced histories: LEARNED receives the correct binding, CONTROL receives matched exposure without it, and WRONG receives a competing relation-valid answer in the same slot. After an identical rehearsal-free continual fine-tuning stream, LEARNED reacquires the target faster than CONTROL at a prespecified checkpoint of partial forgetting.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMachine Learning (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new)\nciteturn41250view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new\",\"pattern\":\"22543\"}); Total lines: 5005\nL225: The web is not static or uniform. Websites that serve the same purpose can look and navigate very differently. Even within a single site, successive redesigns can change how familiar features are presented and accessed. Humans adapt to this by recognizing familiar interaction patterns and reusing what they already know. We give web agents a similar ability with WebMix, a mixture hypernetwork that adapts them to unseen interfaces by composing previously trained low-rank adapters.\nL226: WebMix begins with a bank of adapters trained on source interfaces. At deployment, a scout agent explores the target interface and summarizes its observations into a compact manual. A lightweight router uses this manual to predict mixing coefficients over the adapter bank, which combine the existing adapters into a new one for inference. Adapting to a new interface takes only a scouting run and a forward pass, without gradient updates on the target.\nL227: With Qwen and Llama models, we evaluate domain transfer on TimeWarp and WebArena-Lite-v2, and version transfer on TimeWarp. In all three settings, WebMix matches or outperforms in-context learning and LoRA baselines, often matching the average success of specialists trained directly on those interfaces. On TimeWarp domain transfer, it gains about 8 percentage points on average over the best baseline with both models.\nL228: These gains do not come at the cost of seen interfaces, where WebMix keeps its lead, and extend to version transfer. Adapting to a new interface can therefore rely on exploration and composition instead of retraining.\nL229: [17] cite122†arXiv:2610.22543 [cite123†pdf , cite124†html , cite125†other ]\nL230: \nL231: \nL232: Title: On the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decoding for Diffusion Language Models via Commit-Order Prediction\nL233: \nL234: cite126†Eliezer Shlomi , cite127†Eliron Rahimi , cite128†Avi Mendelson , cite129†Chaim Baskin L235: \nL236: Comments: Preprint, project page: cite130†this https URL†eliezer318.github.io L237: \nL238: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL239: Diffusion language models (DLMs) promise parallel text generation, but in practice face an unresolved speed-quality trade-off. Exact decoding fills in only one masked position per model pass, while faster multi-token decoding schemes are lossy. Speculative decoding is the standard lossless acceleration for autoregressive models. However, it does not transfer directly to DLMs, which must choose both what to generate and where.\nL240: We show that this order is the missing piece: the model's own predictions from the previous pass already give the right token at 92-100% of positions, but not which position comes next. Based on this observation, we introduce Speculating Positions Order Trajectory (SPOT), which learns to predict this order.\nL241: A 0.62M-parameter model reads only the target's output distributions and proposes a tree of candidate continuations, and the target verifies all of them in a single pass by checking whether each equals its own next state. On the verification side, our analysis shows that prior block-diffusion verification approaches either assume $T=0$ or a sequential proposal interface.\nL242: To support tree-based speculation at any temperature, we propose using coupled-noise match verification for diffusion blocks, following shared-Gumbel-noise coupling approaches. Our comprehensive experiments show that SPOT cuts target passes by up to 6.8x and reaches up to 3.2x the throughput of standard decoding, beating the calibrated and training-free drafting baselines in every task and budget at $T=0$ and $T=1$.\nL243: In addition, we show that the learned drafter transfers across models, demonstrating successful transfer across six different DLMs. Project page: cite130†this https URL†eliezer318.github.io L244: [18] cite131†arXiv:2610.22564 [cite132†pdf , cite133†html , cite134†other ]\nL245: \nL246: \nL247: Title: Deterministic Bounds on Accuracy and Stability of Metric-Space Regression and Classification\nL248: \nL249: cite135†Nina Maria Gottschling , cite136†Jakob Gawlikowski , cite137†Michele Caprio L250: \nL251: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\nL252: Data-driven approaches often face ambiguity in datasets due to sensor or target noise, insufficient information, and noise in the input data. While best-fit machine learning methods can accommodate such noise through uncertainty estimates, they are prone to overfitting and it is challenging to quantify ambiguity of the relation between inputs and targets. We introduce a geometric framework that quantifies this ambiguity through the average kernel size, computed with data-dependent, i.e.\nL253: model-independent, feasible sets derived from worst-case input and target noise. The framework provides computable, deterministic, non-asymptotic bounds on the best achievable empirical error and accuracy for a finite, constructed dataset, independent of the predictive model class, and applies to regression and classification with pseudo-metric input spaces and metric target spaces.\n"
